{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_49","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba cli/curator.md | sed -n '90,190p'\"","aggregated_output":"    90\t  - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n    91\t  - name: Install Curator v0.1.1\n    92\t    run: go install github.com/relux-works/curator/cmd/curator@v0.1.1\n    93\t  - name: Materialize skills\n    94\t    run: curator install . --strict-tags\n    95\t  - name: Verify installed state\n    96\t    run: curator status . --check\n    97\t```\n    98\t\n    99\tOrganizations normally install enforced source, audit, and registry policy\n   100\tbefore this job. CI MUST NOT disable a policy required on developer machines.\n   101\t\n   102\t## External repository lifecycle\n   103\t\n   104\tSchema-7 external build repositories use\n   105\t[manager-profile section 11](../profiles/manager.md#11-external-repository-manager-profile).\n   106\tThis is the rc.5 reference CLI contract; a Curator binary that\n   107\tclaims only rc.4 does not yet expose `repair` or accept schema-7 objects. The\n   108\tcommand names below do not change lifecycle ordering:\n   109\t\n   110\t```bash\n   111\t# Syntax and schema validation may run without source coverage.\n   112\tcurator skill check ./skill --json\n   113\t\n   114\t# A dry run may fetch into removable private state and audit exact source, but\n   115\t# creates no persistent repository, snapshot, receipt, cache, marker, or shim.\n   116\tcurator install . --dry-run --audit strict\n   117\t\n   118\t# A real install must resolve and independently audit every external source\n   119\t# before artifact-cache lookup or compilation.\n   120\tcurator install . --audit strict\n   121\t\n   122\t# Status is read-only and never contacts a remote just to re-test a tag.\n   123\tcurator status . --check --json\n   124\t\n   125\t# Repair repeats exact acquisition, audit, and transaction publication.\n   126\tcurator repair . --audit strict\n   127\t\n   128\t# GC retains every marker/journal root and uncertain protected entry.\n   129\tcurator gc\n   130\t```\n   131\t\n   132\tFor an unsubstituted declaration with `tag`, install and repair fetch only\n   133\t`refs/tags/<tag>` into the fixed private destination and prove its terminal\n   134\tcommit equals the immutable lock. They never try a direct object ID, branch,\n   135\tall-tags fetch, or alternate ref. An untagged declaration fetches only its full\n   136\tlocked object ID.\n   137\t\n   138\t`skill check` may report state `unverified-offline`, severity `warning`, and\n   139\tcode `build_repository_unverified_offline` when it is intentionally\n   140\tsyntax-only and cannot obtain the exact snapshot. It must not report source,\n   141\taudit, cache, receipt, artifact, marker, or installation coverage.\n   142\t\n   143\t`install`, `upgrade`, `update`, `repair`, and coverage-claiming `audit` are\n   144\tnever syntax-only, including when `install` or `upgrade` uses `--dry-run`.\n   145\tWithout exact source they report state `blocked`, severity `error`, and code\n   146\t`build_repository_source_unavailable`, return exit code 1, and make no\n   147\tmutation. A protected snapshot does not turn that failure into offline warning\n   148\tsuccess or replace an exact-tag assertion.\n   149\t\n   150\tInstall-family dry-run prints one state per active build command:\n   151\t\n   152\t```text\n   153\tgolden-tool: cache-hit\n   154\tgolden-tool: would-preflight-and-build\n   155\tgolden-tool: would-rebuild-untrusted-cache\n   156\tgolden-tool: corrupt\n   157\tgolden-tool: unsupported\n   158\tgolden-tool: blocked\n   159\t```\n   160\t\n   161\tOnly one state is emitted for a command. `cache-hit` means the exact external\n   162\tsnapshot was raw-object proved, hashed, independently audited, and matched to a\n   163\tvalid protected receipt and artifact. `unverified-offline` is not an install,\n   164\tupgrade, update, repair, or coverage-audit dry-run state.\n   165\t\n   166\tHuman wording may add sanitized detail after the manager profile's stable\n   167\ttuple. JSON output uses the same states and stable codes from manager-profile\n   168\tsection 11.10. Each ordered result contains `command`, `repository`, `phase`,\n   169\t`state`, `severity`, and `code`; `severity` and `code` are `null` only for a\n   170\tsuccessful result. The top-level `status` is exactly `success`, `warning`,\n   171\t`failed`, or `partial`:\n   172\t\n   173\t```json\n   174\t{\n   175\t  \"status\": \"failed\",\n   176\t  \"results\": [\n   177\t    {\n   178\t      \"command\": \"golden-tool\",\n   179\t      \"repository\": \"golden-tools\",\n   180\t      \"phase\": \"source\",\n   181\t      \"state\": \"blocked\",\n   182\t      \"severity\": \"error\",\n   183\t      \"code\": \"build_repository_ref_moved\"\n   184\t    }\n   185\t  ]\n   186\t}\n   187\t```\n   188\t\n   189\tResults use planned command order. Human and JSON diagnostics never expose\n   190\tcredentials, private keys, broker output, SSH agent paths, or unsanitized\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
