{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_54","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"task-board resource get TASK-260720-1nvomm TASK-260720-1nvomm_external-build-repositories-architecture-v6.md --output - | nl -ba | sed -n '821,1020p'\"","aggregated_output":"   821\t\n   822\t- loose objects at\n   823\t  `objects/[0-9a-f]{2}/[0-9a-f]{38}` for SHA-1 or\n   824\t  `objects/[0-9a-f]{2}/[0-9a-f]{62}` for SHA-256;\n   825\t- zero or more exact\n   826\t  `objects/pack/pack-<full-object-format-hex>.pack` and matching `.idx` pairs.\n   827\t\n   828\tEvery loose/pack/index entry is a singly linked regular file. A pack without\n   829\tone matching index, duplicate basename, unexpected suffix, or hash-family\n   830\tmismatch rejects. V1 rejects all other object sidecars/state, including:\n   831\t\n   832\t```text\n   833\tobjects/info/*\n   834\tobjects/pack/*.promisor\n   835\tobjects/pack/*.keep\n   836\tobjects/pack/*.bitmap\n   837\tobjects/pack/*.rev\n   838\tobjects/pack/*.mtimes\n   839\tobjects/pack/multi-pack-index*\n   840\tshallow\n   841\tinfo/grafts\n   842\t```\n   843\t\n   844\tIt also rejects alternates/http-alternates, cruft/promisor packs, namespaces,\n   845\tand unexpected object directories. The source `.idx` is captured only as\n   846\tuntrusted inert input. Manager code validates its fanout, sorted names,\n   847\toffsets, object count, pack checksum, and index checksum against the paired\n   848\tpack before using an offset.\n   849\t\n   850\tThe portable local v1 subset is exact:\n   851\t\n   852\t1. `H` is 20 for SHA-1 and 32 for SHA-256. Every object ID and checksum below\n   853\t   has exactly `H` bytes and uses that repository hash.\n   854\t2. A `.pack` begins with ASCII `PACK`, unsigned 32-bit big-endian version `2`\n   855\t   or `3`, and an unsigned 32-bit big-endian object count `N`. Its last `H`\n   856\t   bytes equal `HASH(all preceding pack bytes)`. The lowercase checksum hex is\n   857\t   exactly the `pack-<hex>` basename.\n   858\t3. A paired `.idx` is version 2 only: bytes `ff 74 4f 63`, unsigned 32-bit\n   859\t   big-endian version `2`, 256 monotonically nondecreasing unsigned 32-bit\n   860\t   fanout entries, and final fanout value `N`.\n   861\t4. The index then contains exactly `N` object names of width `H`, strictly\n   862\t   increasing by unsigned byte order; `N` unsigned 32-bit CRC32 entries; and\n   863\t   `N` unsigned 32-bit offsets. An offset with its high bit clear is a pack\n   864\t   offset. An offset with its high bit set indexes the following table of\n   865\t   unsigned 64-bit big-endian large offsets after clearing that bit. If any\n   866\t   large offset is referenced, the referenced indices are unique and exactly\n   867\t   the dense range `0..M-1`; otherwise `M` is zero. The table contains exactly\n   868\t   `M` entries. Every resolved pack offset is unique, is at least 12, and\n   869\t   begins before the pack trailer. Sorting the resolved offsets defines each\n   870\t   packed-entry byte range through the next offset or the pack trailer; the\n   871\t   corresponding index CRC32 MUST equal zlib CRC32 over that complete range.\n   872\t5. The index ends with the paired pack checksum followed by an index checksum\n   873\t   equal to `HASH(all preceding index bytes)`. There are no omitted or trailing\n   874\t   bytes, duplicate object names, duplicate offsets, decreasing fanout values,\n   875\t   count mismatches, or cross-family hash widths.\n   876\t6. Pack versions other than 2/3, index versions other than 2, version-1\n   877\t   indexes, unknown sidecars, checksum failures, or malformed tables all fail the single stable error\n   878\t   `build_repository_local_object_format_unsupported`.\n   879\t\n   880\tThis admission parser validates the physical container only; it does not trust\n   881\tthe index's claim about an object's identity or decompress source-selected\n   882\tobjects itself. After validation, the manager creates a fresh private bare\n   883\trepository using section 6.2 with the admitted object format, copies only the\n   884\tvalidated loose objects and paired `.pack`/`.idx` files beneath its own\n   885\t`objects/` directory with no-follow/rooted writes, and seals that directory\n   886\tread-only for the object reader. It writes manager-generated repository config\n   887\tand no source config, refs, hooks, attributes, LFS data, alternates, commit\n   888\tgraph, multi-pack index, bitmap, reverse index, or maintenance state.\n   889\t\n   890\tThe manager then uses the common trusted `cat-file` contract in section 6.6.\n   891\tAn OFS_DELTA base remains within its paired pack; a REF_DELTA base may be in\n   892\tanother admitted pack or loose object in the same sealed private store. No base\n   893\tmay come from an alternate, lazy/promisor fetch, source worktree, or network.\n   894\tMissing/cyclic/oversized delta resolution makes `cat-file` fail and therefore\n   895\tfails complete-source proof; the manager never widens the store.\n   896\tEvery consumed object is independently recomputed as:\n   897\t\n   898\t```text\n   899\tobject_id = HASH(type || \" \" || decimal_byte_length || NUL || exact_content)\n   900\t```\n   901\t\n   902\tunder SHA-1 or SHA-256 and MUST equal both the requested and returned full ID.\n   903\tThus trusted Git performs bounded decompression/delta resolution from inert\n   904\tbytes, while manager code retains authority over identity, graph semantics, and\n   905\tmaterialized snapshot bytes. An implementation unable to validate or stage\n   906\tthis exact subset MUST reject it; it MUST NOT invoke the source repository's\n   907\tGit configuration or select a private implementation-specific subset.\n   908\t\n   909\t### 6.6 Common raw-object reader and complete-object proof\n   910\t\n   911\tNetwork fetches already end in a manager-created private repository. Local\n   912\tsubstitutions are copied into an equivalent manager-created private repository\n   913\tunder section 6.5.3. From this point the two source kinds use one contract.\n   914\t\n   915\t#### 6.6.1 Exact `cat-file --batch` process\n   916\t\n   917\tAfter all physical-store checks and before audit or artifact-cache lookup, the\n   918\tmanager launches exactly one direct child:\n   919\t\n   920\t```json\n   921\t[\n   922\t  \"<absolute-trusted-git>\",\n   923\t  \"--git-dir=<operation-private>/repo.git\",\n   924\t  \"--no-replace-objects\",\n   925\t  \"--no-lazy-fetch\",\n   926\t  \"--no-optional-locks\",\n   927\t  \"-c\",\n   928\t  \"core.hooksPath=<operation-private>/empty-hooks\",\n   929\t  \"-c\",\n   930\t  \"core.fsmonitor=false\",\n   931\t  \"-c\",\n   932\t  \"core.untrackedCache=false\",\n   933\t  \"-c\",\n   934\t  \"maintenance.auto=false\",\n   935\t  \"cat-file\",\n   936\t  \"--batch=%(objectname) %(objecttype) %(objectsize)\"\n   937\t]\n   938\t```\n   939\t\n   940\tThe child uses section 6.1's empty CWD and clean environment. In particular\n   941\t`GIT_NO_REPLACE_OBJECTS=1`, `GIT_NO_LAZY_FETCH=1`,\n   942\t`GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS=0`, empty global/system config, no alternates, no worktree,\n   943\tno object-directory override, no credential/SSH/HTTP variables, and a\n   944\tmanager-owned empty `PATH` remain in force. Its repository and object store are\n   945\tread-only. Standard error is bounded diagnostic data. The child has a\n   946\tdeadline, output/object/aggregate-byte limits, process/memory/disk limits, and\n   947\tOS network denial. It may not create a child process or modify repository,\n   948\taudit, cache, or installation state.\n   949\t\n   950\tThe exact negative option contract is equally normative: the argv contains no\n   951\t`--batch-command`, `--batch-all-objects`, `--buffer`, `--follow-symlinks`,\n   952\t`--unordered`, `--textconv`, `--filters`, `--filter`, `--use-mailmap`,\n   953\t`--mailmap`, `--path`, `-Z`, or `-z`. There is no `git ls-tree`, `rev-parse`,\n   954\t`show`, `archive`, `checkout`, `read-tree`, `verify-pack`, or source-selected\n   955\tGit command. Absence of the transformation flags means `cat-file --batch`\n   956\treturns raw uncompressed object contents, not checkout/filter/textconv/mailmap\n   957\toutput.\n   958\t\n   959\tThe manager sends requests sequentially. Each request is exactly:\n   960\t\n   961\t```text\n   962\t<full-lowercase-object-id> LF\n   963\t```\n   964\t\n   965\tThe ID is 40 lowercase hexadecimal bytes for SHA-1 or 64 for SHA-256 and is\n   966\teither the already validated selected ID or an ID parsed from a recomputed\n   967\tcommit, tag, or tree object. No abbreviated ID, whitespace, object/path suffix,\n   968\trevision operator, ref, command word, NUL, CR, or second line is admitted.\n   969\t\n   970\tFor each request, stdout is parsed as exactly:\n   971\t\n   972\t```text\n   973\t<same-full-lowercase-object-id> SP <type> SP <decimal-size> LF\n   974\t<exactly decimal-size content bytes> LF\n   975\t```\n   976\t\n   977\t`type` is one ASCII token from `commit`, `tag`, `tree`, or `blob`. Size is `0`\n   978\tor `[1-9][0-9]*`, fits unsigned 64-bit and manager policy, and has no sign or\n   979\tleading zero. The returned ID MUST byte-equal the request. The manager reads\n   980\texactly the declared content size and one terminal LF before issuing another\n   981\trequest. `missing`, `ambiguous`, `excluded`, `submodule`, unexpected type,\n   982\tmalformed/truncated/extra bytes, early EOF, trailing output after stdin close,\n   983\tnonzero exit, timeout, or limit violation fails\n   984\t`build_repository_incomplete_source`. Stdin closes after the last required\n   985\tobject; a clean child exits zero.\n   986\t\n   987\tThe manager bounds unique object count, individual object bytes, total expanded\n   988\tbytes, tree depth, path depth/length, tag peel depth, diagnostics, and wall\n   989\ttime. Duplicate graph references reuse already verified bytes by full ID and\n   990\tdo not consume another logical object slot. Limits are manager policy with\n   991\tportable minimums fixed by conformance; exceeding any limit fails before audit\n   992\tand never creates a partial snapshot claim.\n   993\t\n   994\t#### 6.6.2 Manager parsing and graph proof\n   995\t\n   996\tFor both network and local sources, manager code—not Git pretty-printers—uses\n   997\tone byte-level semantic parser after object-ID recomputation. Let `H` be 40\n   998\tlowercase hexadecimal bytes for SHA-1 and 64 for SHA-256. The parser applies\n   999\tthe same limits and result codes regardless of acquisition path.\n  1000\t\n  1001\tThe common **header-record grammar** is:\n  1002\t\n  1003\t1. An object is bounded before parsing and contains no NUL byte anywhere.\n  1004\t2. Its header block uses LF only. CR is forbidden before the header/body\n  1005\t   separator. The separator is the first exact `LF LF` and is required even\n  1006\t   when the message is empty. A lone final header LF is not enough.\n  1007\t3. A primary header line is\n  1008\t   `<key> SP <value> LF`. `key` is one or more ASCII letters, digits, or `-`,\n  1009\t   begins with an ASCII letter, and has no normalization. `value` is a bounded\n  1010\t   byte string with no LF, CR, or NUL and may be empty only for an\n  1011\t   implementation-ignored extra header.\n  1012\t4. Zero or more continuation lines may follow an extra header only. Each is\n  1013\t   `SP <continuation-bytes> LF`; the continuation bytes may be empty and\n  1014\t   contain no LF, CR, or NUL. A continuation before the first header or after a\n  1015\t   required structural header is invalid. This admits ordinary `gpgsig`,\n  1016\t   `gpgsig-sha256`, and `mergetag` records without interpreting or verifying\n  1017\t   their signatures.\n  1018\t5. Bytes after the separator are a bounded opaque message. They may contain\n  1019\t   LF, CR, non-UTF-8 bytes, and signature armor, but not NUL. Message bytes\n  1020\t   never select a ref, tree, target, cache key field, credential, or process.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
